-- ─── Lesson 1 · Foundations ───
('wisdom', 'how-to-read-a-teacher',
 'How to Read a Great Teacher',
 'Take the practice, test the claim, leave the metaphysics on the shelf',
 'Nine books and teachers, one rule for reading them: every idea is a tool with a job, and every tool gets tested against your own week. This lesson sets the frame the next ten use.',
 'strong', 5,
 ARRAY['A reading method that keeps the useful part and drops the rest','The honesty contract: what the evidence chips mean when the source is a philosopher, not a study','A one-page map of the nine sources and which lever each one pulls'],
 ARRAY['Reading about practices is not practising them; the lessons only pay off at the level of the try-today steps','Some sources make claims about physics or healing that the evidence does not support; this course says so where it happens'],
 E'Every year a few books change the way millions of people talk about their lives. Some deserve it. The trap is not that these teachers are wrong. It is that a reader swallows the whole package, the practice and the metaphysics together, and then abandons both the first time the metaphysics fails a reality check.\n\n**Ideas are tools, not beliefs**\nA tool has a job. A hammer is not true or false; it is useful or not for the nail in front of you. Read every teacher in this course the same way. Atomic Habits gives you a way to design a cue. Tolle gives you a way to step out of a thought spiral in sixty seconds. Jung gives you a way to notice what you are hiding from yourself. You do not need to believe anything to use any of them. You need to try them on a real Tuesday.\n\n**Three sentences to finish for every source**\n1. The one idea: what does this teacher say that nobody else in the course says?\n2. The practice: what would I actually do, today, for five minutes, if I took it seriously?\n3. The claim I am not buying: what does the source assert that goes beyond what it can show?\n\nIf you can finish all three, you have read the book better than most people who finished it.\n\n**What the evidence chips mean here**\nIn the Longevity course a chip rates studies. Here the sources are often philosophers and practitioners, so the chip rates the *practice*, not the worldview. Strong means the practice has repeated human trials behind it (morning light, a habit cue, slow exhale breathing). Promising means the mechanism is plausible and early studies agree (presence practice, identity-based habits, mental rehearsal). Speculative means the practice may be valuable and the explanation offered for it is not supported: quantum healing, the universe answering, the shadow as a literal entity. Speculative is not an insult. It is a label that lets you keep the exercise and drop the story.\n\n**The nine, and the lever each one pulls**\n- James Clear, Atomic Habits: behaviour design. The lever is your environment and your identity.\n- Eckhart Tolle, The Power of Now: attention. The lever is the gap between a thought and you.\n- Eckhart Tolle, A New Earth: the ego. The lever is noticing when you defend a self-image instead of a fact.\n- Rhonda Byrne, The Greatest Secret: awareness. The lever is welcoming a feeling instead of fighting it.\n- Robin Sharma, Wealth Money Can''t Buy: definition. The lever is measuring a life on eight scales, not one.\n- Carl Jung: the shadow. The lever is projection, what irritates you in others.\n- Joe Dispenza: rehearsal. The lever is practising a future state on purpose.\n- Andrew Huberman: physiology. The lever is light, breath, temperature and dopamine.\n- Alan Watts: security. The lever is the backwards law, letting go of the grip that makes you sink.\n\n**How to use the course**\nOne lesson a day is plenty. Do the try-today step before you read the next one. The recap in Lesson 11 turns the nine practices into one seven-day stack, and it only works if you have run each part once on its own.',
 'Your check-ins, streaks and sessions are all practices. This course adds nine more from people who have shaped how millions think about habits, presence and meaning, with the discipline to say which parts are practice and which parts are story.',
 ARRAY['Read a teacher for the practice you can run this week, not the worldview you must adopt.','Three sentences per source: the one idea, the practice, the claim you are not buying.','Here the evidence chip rates the practice, not the philosophy; speculative means keep the exercise, drop the explanation.'],
 ARRAY['Pick the one source on the list you already know best and write its three sentences before reading its lesson.','Decide the time of day the try-today steps will get their five minutes this week.'],
 '[{"author": "Note", "title": "Original lessons on the ideas of nine published sources; not affiliated with any author or publisher"}, {"author": "Lally, P. et al.", "title": "How are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world (Eur. J. Soc. Psychol.)", "year": 2010}, {"author": "Sagan, C.", "title": "The Demon-Haunted World: Science as a Candle in the Dark", "year": 1995}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "What does a speculative chip mean in this course?", "choices": ["The practice is worthless", "The practice may be valuable but the explanation offered for it is not supported", "The teacher is being dishonest"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Speculative rates the story, not the exercise. Keep the practice, drop the explanation."}, {"q": "Which three sentences should you finish for every source?", "choices": ["The plot, the author, the year", "The one idea, the practice, the claim you are not buying", "The price, the length, the rating"], "correct": 1, "explain": "That is the reading method: it keeps the useful part and names what you are leaving on the shelf."}]'::jsonb,
 300, 1, 'foundations'),

-- ─── Lesson 2 · Foundations ───
('wisdom', 'atomic-habits-identity',
 'Atomic Habits: Identity Before Outcome',
 'James Clear''s system: tiny changes, environment design and a vote for who you are',
 'Clear''s argument is that outcomes follow systems and systems follow identity. Each small action is a vote for the kind of person you are; design the cue and the environment and the votes add up.',
 'promising', 6,
 ARRAY['A four-step loop you can use to build or break any habit','Environment design, the lever most people never touch','The identity frame that makes streaks feel like evidence rather than pressure'],
 ARRAY['One percent better every day is a metaphor, not a measurement; progress is lumpy and plateaus are normal','Habit systems can become their own obsession; the point is the life the habit serves'],
 E'James Clear''s book is the most practical thing ever written about habits, and its central move is easy to miss under the tactics: stop aiming at outcomes and start aiming at identity.\n\n**Outcomes, systems, identity**\nMost goals are outcomes: lose five kilos, run a marathon, read more. Outcomes are where results show up, but they are not where change happens. Change happens in the system, the daily process, and the system is driven by identity, the story you tell about the kind of person you are. A person who *is* a runner runs on a wet Tuesday. A person who *wants to* run negotiates. Clear''s framing is that every action is a vote for a type of person. You do not need a unanimous election. You need a majority.\n\n**The loop: cue, craving, response, reward**\nA habit runs as a loop. A cue (the phone on the pillow) triggers a craving (a hit of novelty), which drives a response (scroll), which delivers a reward (the hit). The loop repeats because the reward teaches the brain to expect it at the cue. Every tactic in the book pulls on one of the four stages.\n\n**The four laws**\n- Make it obvious: put the cue in your path. Shoes by the door, water bottle on the desk, the app on the home screen. Habit stacking is a cue rule: after an existing habit, do the new one.\n- Make it attractive: pair it with something you want. Podcast only while walking.\n- Make it easy: shrink it until it takes two minutes. Not "read a book", "read one page". The two-minute version is not the goal; it is the entrance.\n- Make it satisfying: give yourself a visible tick. A streak is exactly this, a reward that arrives before the outcome does.\n\nBreaking a habit inverts the laws: make it invisible, unattractive, hard, unsatisfying. Move the app off the home screen. Add friction.\n\n**Environment beats willpower**\nThe most under-used lever in the book is the room. People with what looks like great self-control mostly live in environments where the tempting thing is not there. Design the room and you stop needing the willpower. This is why the app asks you to set a session reminder and a check-in time: they are cues placed in your path.\n\n**What the evidence supports**\nHabit research is real and reasonably consistent. Lally and colleagues tracked people forming a new daily behaviour and found automaticity built over roughly two to eight months, not three weeks, with wide individual variation. Missing a single day made no measurable difference to the curve. Wood and Neal''s work shows that a large share of daily behaviour is cued by context rather than chosen, which is the environment argument. What is not evidenced is the tidy arithmetic of one percent per day; treat it as a picture of compounding, not a formula.\n\n**Where it goes wrong**\nTracking becomes the habit. The streak becomes the goal. If you notice you are protecting a number rather than becoming a person, return to the identity question: what would the person I am becoming do today? Clear''s rule for a broken streak is the useful one: never miss twice. One miss is an accident. Two is the start of a new habit.',
 'The streak on your Home screen, the check-in cue and the session reminder are the four laws made concrete. Knowing the system behind them turns the app from a scoreboard into an identity you are casting votes for.',
 ARRAY['Outcomes follow systems and systems follow identity; every action is a vote for a type of person.','Cue, craving, response, reward. Every tactic works on one of the four stages.','Design the room, not the willpower; and never miss twice.'],
 ARRAY['Write one identity sentence for this app: "I am the kind of person who …" and put it where you will read it at your check-in time.','Pick one habit and shrink it to a two-minute version you cannot fail at. Do only that version for a week.','Move one temptation out of your path tonight: the app off the home screen, the snack off the counter.'],
 '[{"author": "Clear, J.", "title": "Atomic Habits", "year": 2018}, {"author": "Note", "title": "An original lesson on the ideas in Atomic Habits; not affiliated with the author or publisher"}, {"author": "Lally, P. et al.", "title": "How are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world", "year": 2010}, {"author": "Wood, W. & Neal, D.", "title": "A new look at habits and the habit-goal interface", "year": 2007}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "In Clear''s model, where does lasting change happen?", "choices": ["In the outcome you set", "In the system and the identity underneath it", "In the app you track it with"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Outcomes are where results show up; systems and identity are where they are produced."}, {"q": "What did Lally''s study find about missing a single day?", "choices": ["It resets the habit to zero", "It made no measurable difference to the automaticity curve", "It doubles the time needed"], "correct": 1, "explain": "One miss does not undo the habit. Clear''s rule follows: never miss twice."}, {"q": "Which is the two-minute rule?", "choices": ["Do every habit for exactly two minutes", "Shrink the habit until starting takes two minutes; the entrance, not the goal", "Rest two minutes between habits"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The tiny version gets you through the door. The full version can follow once showing up is automatic."}]'::jsonb,
 301, 2, 'foundations'),

-- ─── Lesson 3 · Protocol ───
('wisdom', 'power-of-now-presence',
 'The Power of Now: The Watcher and the Pain-Body',
 'Eckhart Tolle''s core move: notice the thinker, and you are already not it',
 'Tolle''s claim is that most suffering is made of thinking about the past and future, and that the way out is not better thoughts but attention to the present moment, where problems are small and action is possible.',
 'promising', 5,
 ARRAY['A sixty-second practice that interrupts rumination without arguing with it','The pain-body idea as a working model for why old emotion returns on a schedule','A clear line between what the research supports and what is Tolle''s philosophy'],
 ARRAY['Presence is not a replacement for planning or for treatment of clinical anxiety and depression','Reading Tolle can turn into a new identity, "the spiritual one"; his later book warns about exactly that'],
 E'Eckhart Tolle''s book became famous for a claim that sounds simple and is not: you are not your thoughts. The voice in your head narrates, judges, replays and rehearses, and most people have never noticed that they can listen to it rather than be it.\n\n**The watcher**\nThe core practice is to notice the thinker. When a thought arrives, especially a repetitive one, you can step back a half-step and observe it: *there is the mind, telling the story about the email again.* The moment you watch a thought, you are no longer fully inside it. Tolle calls this presence. Psychologists call something close to it decentering, and it is the working ingredient in mindfulness training that has held up in trials.\n\n**Time as the problem**\nTolle''s second claim is that almost all psychological suffering is about time: regret is the past, anxiety is the future. The present moment, he argues, is almost always bearable. Try it: the situation right now, this breath, this chair, is usually fine. The unbearable part is the story about what it means for tomorrow. This is not a licence to ignore tomorrow. It is the observation that action only ever happens now, and that a mind that lives in tomorrow does not act, it worries.\n\n**The pain-body**\nTolle''s most useful and most speculative idea. He describes an accumulated store of old emotional pain that wakes up periodically, feeds on drama, and then goes quiet. Whether or not you accept the entity language, the pattern is recognisable: a small trigger produces a huge, familiar reaction that seems to want more of itself. The practice is the same as for thoughts. Notice it, name it, do not feed it. Naming an emotion, in the affect-labelling studies, measurably lowers its intensity.\n\n**What the evidence supports**\nA large experience-sampling study by Killingsworth and Gilbert found that people''s minds wander close to half the time, and that a wandering mind predicts lower mood in the next moment, regardless of what the person is doing. Mindfulness-based programmes that train present-moment attention show moderate, reliable effects on anxiety, depression and stress in meta-analyses. Affect labelling reduces amygdala response. So the practice is promising to strong. The metaphysics, presence as a spiritual awakening and the pain-body as a semi-autonomous entity, are Tolle''s own and stay speculative.\n\n**The sixty-second return**\nStop. Feel your hands from the inside. Hear the furthest sound in the room. Take one slow breath out, longer than in. Ask: what is actually here, right now? Then continue. Sixty seconds, no app, no cushion. Run it three times a day for a week before you decide what you think about Tolle.',
 'Evening reflection and the check-in both ask you to look at your day rather than be swept by it. The watcher is that skill in its purest form, and it is trainable in sixty-second doses.',
 ARRAY['You can watch a thought instead of being it; that half-step is the whole practice.','Most suffering lives in past and future; action only ever happens now.','The pain-body is a useful pattern: a small trigger, a huge familiar reaction that wants to be fed. Name it, do not feed it.'],
 ARRAY['Run the sixty-second return three times today: hands, furthest sound, one long exhale, "what is here right now?"','Next time a familiar over-reaction starts, silently name it ("the old anger") and watch what happens to it over two minutes.','Notice one moment today when the situation was fine and the story about it was not.'],
 '[{"author": "Tolle, E.", "title": "The Power of Now", "year": 1997}, {"author": "Note", "title": "An original lesson on the ideas in The Power of Now; not affiliated with the author or publisher"}, {"author": "Killingsworth, M. & Gilbert, D.", "title": "A wandering mind is an unhappy mind (Science)", "year": 2010}, {"author": "Lieberman, M. et al.", "title": "Putting feelings into words: affect labeling disrupts amygdala activity", "year": 2007}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "What is the core practice of The Power of Now?", "choices": ["Replacing negative thoughts with positive ones", "Noticing the thinker, so you observe a thought rather than being inside it", "Planning the future in more detail"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Tolle does not ask you to win the argument with the mind. He asks you to watch it."}, {"q": "What did Killingsworth and Gilbert find?", "choices": ["Minds rarely wander", "A wandering mind predicts lower mood in the next moment", "Daydreaming improves mood"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Their experience-sampling study is the strongest single piece of evidence for training present-moment attention."}]'::jsonb,
 302, 3, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 4 · Protocol ───
('wisdom', 'new-earth-ego',
 'A New Earth: Life Beyond the Ego',
 'Tolle''s second book: the ego is identification, and you can catch it in the act',
 'A New Earth makes the ego concrete: it is the mind identifying with a role, a possession, an opinion or a grievance. The practice is catching that identification in the moment it defends itself.',
 'speculative', 5,
 ARRAY['A working definition of ego you can actually observe: identification, not arrogance','The three tells: being right, being wronged, and needing to be more','An honest view of what is philosophy and what overlaps with self-distancing research'],
 ARRAY['Ego is not a diagnosis to apply to other people; used that way it is the ego doing the diagnosing','Dissolving the sense of self is not a treatment goal for anyone in a fragile mental state; presence work belongs alongside care, not instead of it'],
 E'If The Power of Now was about the thinker, A New Earth is about what the thinker builds: a self made of identifications. Tolle''s ego is not vanity. It is the mind''s habit of saying *I am* to things that are not you: a job title, a body shape, an opinion, a country, a grievance, a follower count.\n\n**Identification**\nWatch what happens when someone criticises something you own. Not you, the thing. A car, a training programme, a team. The reaction is out of proportion because the mind has folded the thing into the self. Tolle''s claim is that all ego works like this, and that the suffering it produces is structural: anything you identify with can be lost, so a self made of identifications is permanently under threat.\n\n**Three tells**\n1. Being right. The ego would rather win an argument than solve a problem. Notice the pull to have the last word, and ask whether you want to be right or want the thing fixed.\n2. Being wronged. Grievance is one of the ego''s favourite foods, because a wound is a very stable identity. "What they did to me" can last decades. The practice is not to deny what happened but to notice when the story has become who you are.\n3. Needing more. The ego cannot arrive; every achievement becomes the new baseline. This is why the finish line moves. Tolle''s answer is not to stop achieving but to stop needing the achievement to complete a self that was never incomplete.\n\n**Roles**\nA New Earth spends a long time on roles: the parent, the expert, the victim, the spiritual person. A role is fine as a function and a trap as an identity. The tell is when you can feel yourself performing the role rather than doing the thing. Tolle''s practice here is deceptively small: catch yourself in the act, and the act loses some of its grip.\n\n**Where the evidence stands**\nThe research overlap is with self-distancing. Ethan Kross and colleagues show that stepping back from the self, even by talking to yourself in the third person, reduces rumination and improves emotion regulation. Studies of self-affirmation show that people defend their positions less rigidly when their sense of worth does not depend on winning. That supports the practice: less identification, less threat, better decisions. The larger claim, that a collective shift in consciousness is arriving, is a spiritual thesis and stays speculative.\n\n**The practice**\nOnce a day, catch one identification. The moment you feel defensive, ask: what did I just make part of me? Not to scold yourself. To notice. Tolle''s whole method rests on the claim that noticing is enough, and for the small daily identifications, the ones that cost you an evening of irritation, it usually is.',
 'Ranks, tiers and boards are built into this app, and they are useful only as long as you do not become them. Catching identification is the skill that lets you compete hard and stay free.',
 ARRAY['Ego is identification: the mind saying "I am" to things that can be lost.','Three tells: needing to be right, needing to be wronged, needing more.','Catch one identification a day; noticing loosens it more than arguing does.'],
 ARRAY['Next time you feel defensive, ask silently: what did I just make part of me?','Pick one grievance you have told more than three times and try telling it once in the third person: "he was hurt when …"','Name one role you perform rather than do, and do the thing once today without the performance.'],
 '[{"author": "Tolle, E.", "title": "A New Earth: Awakening to Your Life''s Purpose", "year": 2005}, {"author": "Note", "title": "An original lesson on the ideas in A New Earth; not affiliated with the author or publisher"}, {"author": "Kross, E. & Ayduk, O.", "title": "Self-distancing: theory, research, and current directions", "year": 2017}, {"author": "Cohen, G. & Sherman, D.", "title": "The psychology of change: self-affirmation and social psychological intervention", "year": 2014}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "How does A New Earth define the ego?", "choices": ["Arrogance and pride", "The mind identifying with things that are not you", "Confidence in your abilities"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Ego here is identification. Anything you fold into the self becomes something you can lose."}, {"q": "Which research overlaps most with Tolle''s dis-identification practice?", "choices": ["Self-distancing and self-affirmation", "Sleep research", "Strength periodisation"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Kross''s self-distancing work shows that stepping back from the self reduces rumination and improves regulation."}]'::jsonb,
 303, 4, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 5 · Protocol ───
('wisdom', 'greatest-secret-awareness',
 'The Greatest Secret: You Are the Awareness',
 'Rhonda Byrne''s second act: not attracting things, but noticing what notices',
 'The Greatest Secret drops the law of attraction and turns to non-duality: you are not the thoughts or feelings but the awareness they appear in. Its one practice, welcoming a feeling instead of fighting it, is worth keeping.',
 'speculative', 4,
 ARRAY['The welcoming practice: a way to sit with a difficult feeling that does not escalate it','A clean separation between this book and the manifestation claims of The Secret','The "am I aware?" inquiry as a two-second reset'],
 ARRAY['The book presents awareness as a metaphysical fact; treat it as a practice frame, not a discovery about the universe','Welcoming a feeling is not the same as accepting a harmful situation; feelings are for welcoming, circumstances are for changing'],
 E'Rhonda Byrne''s first book, The Secret, told millions that thoughts attract outcomes. The Inner Work pillar of this Vault has an honest lesson on why that claim fails (manifestation-demystified). Her later book is a different animal. The Greatest Secret says the thing you have been looking for is not a thing at all: it is the awareness you have been looking with.\n\n**The claim**\nThoughts come and go. Feelings come and go. The body changes. What stays constant through all of it is the fact that it is being noticed. Byrne, drawing on the non-dual teachers she quotes throughout, argues that this noticing is what you actually are, and that suffering comes from mistaking yourself for the contents instead of the container.\n\nYou do not have to accept the metaphysics to test the observation. Ask, right now: am I aware? The answer is obviously yes, and it arrived before any thought did. That two-second question is the book''s most portable practice. It does not solve anything; it briefly moves you out of the argument in your head into the room you are in.\n\n**Welcoming**\nThe practice this lesson keeps is the one for difficult feelings. Most of us do one of two things with a hard emotion: push it down or feed it a story. Byrne''s alternative is to let it be there fully, as a sensation in the body, without the narrative, and to notice that a feeling that is allowed tends to pass in minutes while a feeling that is fought can last for days. This maps closely onto what acceptance-based therapies train. In ACT and in exposure work, willingness to feel an emotion without trying to control it is the ingredient that reduces its power over behaviour.\n\n**What is supported and what is not**\nSupported: allowing an emotion rather than suppressing it. Suppression studies consistently show a rebound, the white bear effect, where the pushed-down thought returns stronger. Accepting a feeling lowers its intensity and its cost. Not supported: that awareness is the fundamental substance of reality, or that realising it removes suffering permanently. Those are claims from a contemplative tradition, and they are honestly labelled speculative here. Keep the practice.\n\n**The five-minute version**\nSit. Find the hardest feeling present today. Drop the story about it and find where it lives in the body. Say, silently, *you can be here.* Breathe with it for five minutes. Notice whether it changed. Most people find it did, and that the change came from not fighting it.',
 'Every check-in asks how you feel, and most of us answer with a fight. Welcoming is the skill that lets a hard day be logged honestly and then let go, instead of carried into tomorrow.',
 ARRAY['The book''s claim: you are the awareness, not the contents; test it with the question "am I aware?"','Welcoming a feeling, as sensation without story, tends to shorten it; fighting it tends to lengthen it.','This is not The Secret: no attraction, no ordering from the universe. The practice stands on its own.'],
 ARRAY['Ask "am I aware?" three times today, especially mid-argument with your own head.','Take one difficult feeling, drop its story, find it in the body, and give it five minutes of "you can be here".','Read the Inner Work lesson manifestation-demystified if you have not, so the two Byrne books stay separate in your mind.'],
 '[{"author": "Byrne, R.", "title": "The Greatest Secret", "year": 2020}, {"author": "Note", "title": "An original lesson on the ideas in The Greatest Secret; not affiliated with the author or publisher"}, {"author": "Hayes, S. et al.", "title": "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy: The Process and Practice of Mindful Change", "year": 2012}, {"author": "Wegner, D.", "title": "Ironic processes of mental control (Psychological Review)", "year": 1994}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "How does The Greatest Secret differ from The Secret?", "choices": ["It is a sequel about attracting more money", "It drops the law of attraction and turns to awareness itself", "It is the same book with new examples"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The first book was about attracting outcomes; the second is about noticing what notices."}, {"q": "What does suppression research show about pushing a feeling down?", "choices": ["It works permanently", "The feeling rebounds stronger", "It has no effect"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Wegner''s ironic-process work: the suppressed content returns. Allowing it is the alternative the practice trains."}]'::jsonb,
 304, 5, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 6 · Protocol ───
('wisdom', 'eight-forms-of-wealth',
 'Wealth Money Can''t Buy: The Eight Forms',
 'Robin Sharma''s audit: growth, wellness, family, craft, money, community, adventure, service',
 'Sharma''s argument is that a life measured on one scale, money, is poor even when the number is high. His eight forms of wealth are a scorecard; the practice is the audit and the rebalancing.',
 'speculative', 5,
 ARRAY['A scorecard that makes "am I doing well?" answerable in five minutes','The lowest-score rule: the weakest form is where the next month of effort goes','Links to the longevity evidence on connection and purpose, which is where the framework is strongest'],
 ARRAY['The eight forms are a framework, not a finding; scores are a prompt for honesty, not a measurement','Rebalancing can become another optimisation project; the point is a life that feels rich, not a spreadsheet that does'],
 E'Ask a room of people whether they are wealthy and most will answer with a number. Robin Sharma''s book is an argument against the number. Not against money, which is one of his eight forms, but against letting one form stand in for the other seven.\n\n**The eight forms**\n1. Growth: are you learning, stretching, becoming someone slightly larger than last year?\n2. Wellness: energy, sleep, strength, the body you live in.\n3. Family: the people who will be at the hospital bed, and whether you are present for them now.\n4. Craft: work you are proud of, done well, whether or not anyone is watching.\n5. Money: enough, with margin, and the freedom that margin buys.\n6. Community: friendships, the neighbours, the people who know your name.\n7. Adventure: novelty, travel, the things you will actually remember.\n8. Service: what you give that is not for you.\n\nSharma''s point is that the forms trade off. Money bought with wellness and family is a loan against the other forms, and the interest is brutal. The forms also compound: a strong community makes adventure easier, service feeds growth, wellness underwrites everything.\n\n**The audit**\nScore each form from one to five, honestly, today. Do not average them. Look at the lowest number. Sharma''s practical rule, and the one this lesson keeps, is that the lowest form is the one that sets the ceiling on how rich the life feels. A person with money at five and family at one does not feel wealthy. So the next month of deliberate effort goes to the lowest form, not the highest.\n\n**Where the evidence is strong**\nThe framework itself is a philosophy of the good life; it is not a study. But two of its forms carry heavy evidence. The Harvard Study of Adult Development, running since 1938, found that the quality of close relationships is the strongest predictor of health and happiness in later life, ahead of wealth and fame. The Longevity course''s lesson on connection and purpose walks through the mortality data: loneliness and lack of purpose carry risks comparable to smoking. So when the audit says community or family is your one, the research says the same thing louder.\n\n**Where it goes wrong**\nTwo ways. First, the audit becomes a project and you start optimising eight scores like a game, which is the money mindset wearing a new coat. Second, you score yourself against Sharma''s image of a rich life instead of your own. Adventure for one person is a mountain; for another it is a new recipe. Score what would feel rich to you, not what would look rich.\n\n**Once a week, five minutes**\nThe audit only works if it repeats. Sunday evening, eight numbers, one lowest. That is the practice the recap lesson builds into the weekly stack.',
 'The W-Index measures the body and the habits. It does not measure family, craft or service. This lesson is the wider scorecard, so a good week in the app can be checked against a good week in the life.',
 ARRAY['Wealth on one scale is poverty on seven; the eight forms are the wider scorecard.','Score each form one to five, then work on the lowest, because the lowest sets how rich the life feels.','Relationships and purpose are where this framework meets the strongest longevity evidence.'],
 ARRAY['Score the eight forms one to five, honestly, now. Circle the lowest.','Write one concrete action for the lowest form that fits in the next seven days.','Put a five-minute "eight forms" audit in your calendar for Sunday evening.'],
 '[{"author": "Sharma, R.", "title": "Wealth Money Can''t Buy: The 8 Hidden Habits to Live Your Richest Life", "year": 2024}, {"author": "Note", "title": "An original lesson on the ideas in Wealth Money Can''t Buy; not affiliated with the author or publisher"}, {"author": "Waldinger, R. & Schulz, M.", "title": "The Good Life: Lessons from the World''s Longest Scientific Study of Happiness", "year": 2023}, {"author": "Holt-Lunstad, J. et al.", "title": "Loneliness and social isolation as risk factors for mortality (Perspectives on Psychological Science)", "year": 2015}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "What is the lowest-score rule?", "choices": ["Ignore the weakest form and build on strengths", "The weakest form sets the ceiling on how rich the life feels, so it gets the next month of effort", "Average the eight scores and track the mean"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Money at five with family at one does not feel wealthy. Rebalancing starts at the bottom."}, {"q": "Which of the eight forms has the strongest longevity evidence behind it?", "choices": ["Adventure", "Community and family (close relationships)", "Craft"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The Harvard study and the loneliness meta-analyses put close relationships ahead of wealth for health and happiness."}]'::jsonb,
