-- ─── Lesson 7 · Protocol ───
('wisdom', 'jung-shadow-individuation',
 'Jung: The Shadow, the Persona and Becoming Whole',
 'Carl Jung''s map of the parts of you that you do not show, and why they run the show anyway',
 'Jung''s shadow is everything you have decided you are not. It does not disappear; it leaks, mostly as strong reactions to other people. The practice is to use those reactions as a map.',
 'speculative', 6,
 ARRAY['The projection test: a reliable way to find your own shadow in five minutes','Persona and shadow as a pair, so the mask makes sense instead of feeling like a lie','Individuation as a direction for the second half of life, not a destination'],
 ARRAY['Jung''s system is depth psychology, not experimental psychology; treat it as a language for self-observation, not a set of proven mechanisms','Shadow work can stir up material that needs a professional; if it does, that is the moment to get one'],
 E'Carl Jung spent his life mapping the parts of the mind that are not on display. Three of his ideas have survived a century because people keep recognising themselves in them: the persona, the shadow, and individuation.\n\n**The persona**\nThe persona is the face you present: competent, calm, kind, funny, strong. Jung did not think it was fake. A social mask is necessary. The problem starts when you mistake the mask for the whole face, and everything the mask does not show has to go somewhere.\n\n**The shadow**\nWhere it goes is the shadow: every quality you decided, usually early and usually without noticing, that you are not. Angry, lazy, needy, ambitious, weak, selfish, soft. The shadow is not only the dark stuff. A person raised to be modest may have their ambition in the shadow; a person raised to be tough may have their tenderness there. Jung''s claim is that what you refuse does not vanish. It leaks: in over-reactions, in the people you cannot stand, in the mistakes you keep making at the worst possible moment.\n\n**Projection: the test**\nHere is the practice, and it is the most reliable thing in Jung. Notice who irritates you out of proportion. Not people who have harmed you; people whose *manner* gets under your skin. The loud one, the show-off, the one who cries easily, the one who never tries. Jung''s proposal is that a disproportionate reaction is a projection: you are seeing your own refused quality on someone else''s face. It is uncomfortable and it is usually right. The practice is to write the irritating quality down and ask where it lives in you, unexpressed or over-controlled.\n\n**Individuation**\nJung''s word for the direction of a life is individuation: becoming the whole person you actually are rather than the half the persona allows. It is not self-improvement. It is integration, letting the refused parts have a legitimate place. The tough person lets tenderness in on purpose. The modest person lets the ambition be seen. Jung thought this was the work of the second half of life, once the persona has done its job of getting you established.\n\n**Where the evidence stands**\nJung''s system is not experimentally tested and mostly cannot be; that is why the chip says speculative. But the pieces line up with modern findings. Thought and emotion suppression produce rebound. People do rate others more negatively on traits they deny in themselves; defensive projection has been demonstrated in controlled studies, if not in Jung''s full form. Self-concept research shows that a narrow self-image is fragile under threat. So the practice, using strong reactions as information, is supported; the architecture of archetypes and the collective unconscious is Jung''s, and you do not need it.\n\n**The practice**\nKeep a short list this week: three people or types who get a disproportionate reaction from you, and the quality in each that does it. Then one honest sentence per quality: where it lives in me. That list is your shadow''s table of contents.',
 'Squads, boards and battles put other people in front of you every day. The ones who get under your skin are not noise; in Jung''s map they are the most precise information about you that you will get this week.',
 ARRAY['The persona is the face you show; the shadow is everything you decided you are not. It leaks.','Disproportionate irritation at someone''s manner is usually projection: your refused quality on their face.','Individuation is integration, not improvement: letting the refused parts have a legitimate place.'],
 ARRAY['List three people or types who irritate you out of proportion, and the quality in each that does it.','For each quality, write one sentence: where does this live in me, unexpressed or over-controlled?','Let one refused quality out on purpose this week, in a small safe way: ask for help, or take the credit.'],
 '[{"author": "Jung, C.G.", "title": "Man and His Symbols", "year": 1964}, {"author": "Jung, C.G.", "title": "Aion: Researches into the Phenomenology of the Self (Collected Works vol. 9ii)", "year": 1951}, {"author": "Note", "title": "An original lesson on Jung''s ideas; not affiliated with any estate or publisher"}, {"author": "Newman, L., Duff, K. & Baumeister, R.", "title": "A new look at defensive projection (JPSP)", "year": 1997}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "What is the shadow, in Jung''s map?", "choices": ["Your evil side", "Every quality you decided you are not, positive or negative, which then leaks", "Your worst memories"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Ambition and tenderness end up in the shadow as often as anger. What is refused does not vanish."}, {"q": "What is the projection test?", "choices": ["Guessing what others think of you", "Noticing who irritates you out of proportion and asking where that quality lives in you", "Projecting confidence in meetings"], "correct": 1, "explain": "A disproportionate reaction to someone''s manner is the most reliable pointer to your own shadow."}]'::jsonb,
 306, 7, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 8 · Protocol ───
('wisdom', 'dispenza-rehearsal',
 'Dispenza: Rehearse the Person You Are Becoming',
 'Joe Dispenza''s method, stripped to what works: mental rehearsal with feeling, and the honest limits',
 'Dispenza teaches meditation as rehearsal: think and feel the future state until the brain and body treat it as familiar. The rehearsal has evidence behind it. The quantum and healing claims do not, and this lesson says so.',
 'speculative', 5,
 ARRAY['A ten-minute rehearsal protocol that uses the evidenced part of the method','The thought-feeling loop as a clear picture of why you keep re-living the same day','A precise line between motor imagery research and the claims that go beyond it'],
 ARRAY['Meditation is not a substitute for medical treatment; claims of healing serious disease through rehearsal are not supported and can be dangerous if they delay care','Elevated emotion on purpose is a skill, not a switch; expect weeks of flat sessions before it comes'],
 E'Joe Dispenza''s books describe a loop most people will recognise: you wake up, think the same thoughts, which produce the same feelings, which drive the same choices, which produce the same day. His claim is that the loop can be broken on purpose, in meditation, by rehearsing a different state until it is more familiar than the old one.\n\n**The loop**\nThoughts produce feelings. Feelings shape what you notice and what you want. Wants drive actions. Actions produce a day, and the day confirms the thoughts. Dispenza''s framing, that you are literally rehearsing your current self every morning, is a useful exaggeration. Habit research agrees with the mechanism: repeated thought-and-response pairs become automatic and context-cued.\n\n**Rehearsal with feeling**\nThe method is to sit, settle, and then rehearse a specific future: a hard set completed, a conversation handled well, a version of you a year from now. Not as a picture only. Dispenza insists on the feeling: the body should experience the state, gratitude or steadiness or pride, *now*, as if the event had already happened. His argument is that the brain does not distinguish well between a vividly rehearsed experience and a lived one, so rehearsal with emotion installs the new pattern.\n\n**What the evidence supports**\nQuite a lot, for the rehearsal. Motor imagery research shows that mentally practising a movement activates overlapping brain regions and produces measurable strength and skill gains, smaller than physical practice but real. The Inner Work lesson visualization-that-works covers the sharper version: rehearse the *process* and the obstacles, not the trophy. Contemplative practice reliably shifts stress physiology, and deliberately generated positive emotion has measurable effects on heart-rate variability and mood. So the ten-minute practice below is promising.\n\n**What it does not support**\nDispenza''s explanation reaches into quantum physics and claims that meditation can cure serious illness. The physics is used as a metaphor and presented as a mechanism; that is not how the field works. The healing testimonials are uncontrolled. This lesson labels the method speculative because of those claims, and asks you to keep the rehearsal and leave the rest. If you are ill, the rehearsal can sit next to treatment. It cannot replace it.\n\n**The ten-minute rehearsal**\nSit. Two minutes of slow breathing, longer out than in. Then pick one specific scene from the next week, not the next decade. Rehearse it from the inside: what you see, what your hands do, the exact moment it gets hard and what you do then. Let the feeling of having done it well arrive in the body and stay with it for two minutes. Open your eyes. Do the real thing within the week, so the rehearsal has somewhere to land.',
 'The rest timer gives you sixty to ninety seconds of nothing between sets. Rehearsing the next set from the inside, feeling included, is the evidenced core of Dispenza''s method in exactly the place it pays.',
 ARRAY['Thoughts, feelings, choices, day: the loop rehearses your current self unless you rehearse another one.','Rehearsal with feeling has real evidence behind it, from motor imagery to emotion regulation.','The quantum and healing claims do not; keep the ten minutes, drop the physics, never delay treatment.'],
 ARRAY['Run the ten-minute rehearsal on one specific scene from the next seven days, obstacle included.','Between sets today, rehearse the next set from the inside for thirty seconds before you lift.','Read the Inner Work lesson visualization-that-works for the process-not-outcome rule the rehearsal needs.'],
 '[{"author": "Dispenza, J.", "title": "Breaking the Habit of Being Yourself", "year": 2012}, {"author": "Note", "title": "An original lesson on Dispenza''s method; not affiliated with the author or publisher. The healing and quantum claims are not supported and are labelled speculative here"}, {"author": "Ranganathan, V. et al.", "title": "From mental power to muscle power: gaining strength by using the mind (Neuropsychologia)", "year": 2004}, {"author": "Oettingen, G.", "title": "Rethinking Positive Thinking", "year": 2014}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "Which part of Dispenza''s method has evidence behind it?", "choices": ["Curing illness through meditation", "Mental rehearsal of a specific state with feeling", "Quantum field explanations"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Motor imagery and emotion-regulation research support the rehearsal. The physics and the healing claims are not supported."}, {"q": "What should you rehearse?", "choices": ["The trophy at the end of the decade", "One specific scene from the next week, including the moment it gets hard", "Nothing specific; a general feeling of success"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Rehearsal works on process and obstacles, and it needs a real event within the week to land in."}]'::jsonb,
 307, 8, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 9 · Protocol ───
('wisdom', 'huberman-protocol-stack',
 'Huberman: Light, Breath, Dopamine, Cold',
 'Andrew Huberman''s most reliable protocols, ranked by the evidence and stacked into a day',
 'Huberman''s gift is turning physiology into protocols. Four of them, morning light, the physiological sigh, dopamine baselining and deliberate cold, carry the strongest evidence and slot into a day without a single new app.',
 'strong', 6,
 ARRAY['Four protocols with the mechanism and the evidence grade next to each','A default day that uses them in the order the biology prefers','Honest notes on where the podcast reaches beyond the studies'],
 ARRAY['Cold exposure is not for everyone: heart conditions, pregnancy and Raynaud''s need a doctor''s go-ahead first','Protocol-stacking can turn a day into a checklist; the point is a body that works, not a body that is managed'],
 E'Andrew Huberman is a Stanford neuroscientist whose podcast made lab findings usable. Not every episode is equal; some rest on one rodent study. These four protocols are the ones where the human evidence is deep enough to call strong or close to it.\n\n**1. Morning light, ten minutes, outside**\nThe mechanism is the circadian clock. Bright light early in the day, ideally within an hour of waking and outdoors even when it is overcast, sets the timer that releases cortisol in the morning and melatonin about fourteen to sixteen hours later. The evidence is strong: light is the dominant zeitgeber in human chronobiology, and morning light exposure improves sleep timing and mood in controlled studies. Windows filter most of the useful intensity. Sunglasses too. Ten minutes on a clear day, twenty when it is grey. This is the first thing in the stack because everything else runs on the clock it sets.\n\n**2. The physiological sigh**\nTwo inhales through the nose, the second one a short top-up, then one long exhale through the mouth. One to three of them. It is the fastest evidenced way to bring arousal down in real time. Huberman''s own lab, with Balban and Spiegel, ran a controlled trial in 2023: five minutes a day of cyclic sighing improved mood and lowered breathing rate more than mindfulness meditation over a month. Use it before a heavy set, after a hard message, at the start of a check-in.\n\n**3. Dopamine baseline**\nHuberman''s framing: dopamine is a currency of motivation, and every cheap hit, the scroll, the sugar, the constant stimulus, spends it against a baseline that then drops. The protocol is to protect the baseline: no phone in the first hour, caffeine delayed ninety minutes so the morning cortisol peak does the waking, and effort itself treated as the reward rather than the thing after. The reward-prediction science behind this is strong. The specific ninety-minute caffeine figure is a reasonable inference from adenosine clearance, not a trial result, so treat it as a default to test.\n\n**4. Deliberate cold**\nA cold shower or plunge, one to three minutes, a few times a week. Cold produces a sustained rise in dopamine and noradrenaline, which is the well-documented part, and a resilience effect that is partly the practice of staying calm under an unpleasant stimulus. Claims about fat loss and immunity are thinner. The rule that matters: cold after strength training blunts muscle adaptation, so keep it away from the hours after a session or put it before.\n\n**The default day**\nWake, light outside, water. No phone until the light is done. Caffeine around ninety minutes in. Three physiological sighs before the day''s first hard thing. Training in the afternoon if you can, cold in the morning or on rest days. Screens dim after sunset, because the same clock that light set in the morning is what late light breaks.\n\n**Where the podcast overreaches**\nSupplements, most of them. Specific timings quoted to the minute. Single-study claims presented as settled. The four protocols above are the ones that survive a sceptical read. The Recovery and Nervous System courses in this Vault cover NSDR and the sleep toolkit in more depth.',
 'Sleep, water and training are three of the numbers your check-in tracks. Morning light and the physiological sigh are the two cheapest levers on them, and they cost ten minutes and three breaths.',
 ARRAY['Morning light outside sets the clock the whole day runs on; strong evidence, ten minutes.','The physiological sigh is the fastest evidenced way to lower arousal in real time.','Protect the dopamine baseline; keep cold away from the hours after strength work.'],
 ARRAY['Tomorrow: ten minutes outside within an hour of waking, no sunglasses, phone in your pocket.','Three physiological sighs before the first hard thing today, and before your first heavy set.','Delay caffeine by ninety minutes for three days and note whether the afternoon dip changes.'],
 '[{"author": "Balban, M.Y. et al.", "title": "Brief structured respiration practices enhance mood and reduce physiological arousal (Cell Reports Medicine)", "year": 2023}, {"author": "Huberman Lab Podcast", "title": "Master Your Sleep & Be More Alert When Awake; Controlling Your Dopamine", "year": 2021}, {"author": "Note", "title": "An original lesson on Huberman''s protocols; not affiliated with the author or the podcast"}, {"author": "Roberts, L.A. et al.", "title": "Post-exercise cold water immersion attenuates acute anabolic signalling and long-term adaptations in muscle (J Physiol)", "year": 2015}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "Why is morning light first in the stack?", "choices": ["It burns calories", "It sets the circadian clock that the rest of the day, including sleep, runs on", "It replaces coffee"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Light is the dominant time-setter for the human clock; melatonin release is timed from the morning signal."}, {"q": "When should cold exposure NOT be done?", "choices": ["Before breakfast", "In the hours after strength training", "On rest days"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Post-training cold blunts the anabolic signalling the session was for. Put it before, or on a rest day."}, {"q": "What did the 2023 Balban trial find?", "choices": ["Cyclic sighing beat mindfulness meditation for mood and arousal over a month", "Breathing has no effect on mood", "Meditation beat breathing"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Five minutes a day of cyclic sighing outperformed meditation on mood and breathing rate."}]'::jsonb,
 308, 9, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 10 · Protocol ───
('wisdom', 'watts-wisdom-of-insecurity',
 'Watts: The Wisdom of Insecurity and the Backwards Law',
 'Alan Watts on why grabbing at security is what makes you sink, and life as something played rather than solved',
 'Watts, the philosopher who brought Eastern thought to the West in plain English, argued that the demand for security is the source of insecurity, and that life is better treated as music than as a journey to a destination.',
 'speculative', 5,
 ARRAY['The backwards law: a single idea that explains why effort at feeling calm produces the opposite','A reframe of progress that keeps you training without turning every week into a means to an end','The overlap with acceptance research, which is where Watts turns out to be right'],
 ARRAY['Watts is a philosopher and a talker, not a therapist; his ideas are a lens, not a treatment','"Life as play" can be misread as "nothing matters"; Watts meant the opposite, that the doing matters and the arriving does not'],
 E'Alan Watts made a career out of explaining Zen and Taoist ideas to Western audiences without the incense. Two of his ideas are worth keeping, and both are useful in exactly the places a driven person gets stuck.\n\n**The backwards law**\nWatts observed that some things get further away the harder you reach for them. Try to fall asleep and you stay awake. Try to feel confident and you become aware of your nerves. Try to be spontaneous and you freeze. His explanation is that the effort to secure a state is itself a signal that the state is absent, and the signal keeps it absent. The picture he uses is water: thrash and you sink, relax and you float.\n\nThis maps closely onto what acceptance-based therapy found decades later. Struggling against anxiety maintains it; willingness to have it reduces its grip. Rumination is the mind trying to solve a feeling. Watts''s version is more elegant: the demand for security *is* the insecurity.\n\n**Life is not a journey**\nWatts''s other great idea is about what a life is for. If it is a journey, the point is the destination, and every present moment becomes a corridor to somewhere else. School was for the job, the job for the promotion, the promotion for retirement, and then you arrive and find there was nothing to arrive at. Watts said life is closer to music. Nobody plays a symphony to reach the final chord. The point is the playing, all the way through.\n\nFor someone who trains, this is not a licence to stop. It is the difference between a session that is only a means to a body, and a session that is also the thing itself: the bar, the breath, the last rep. The first version burns out when the body does not arrive fast enough. The second lasts decades. The Longevity course''s marginal decade is a destination; Watts is the reminder that the forty years of training before it are not a corridor.\n\n**Where the evidence stands**\nWatts made no empirical claims and would have laughed at being tested, which is why the chip says speculative. But the backwards law is close to a finding. Experiential avoidance, the effort to control or escape internal experience, predicts anxiety, depression and poorer outcomes across many studies. Acceptance predicts the reverse. Intrinsic motivation, doing the thing for itself, predicts persistence better than extrinsic reward. So the two ideas, stop grabbing and play the piece, have research standing behind them even if Watts never asked for it.\n\n**The practice**\nOnce a day, do one thing entirely for its own sake, with no intended result: a walk that goes nowhere, a set you do not log, a conversation with no agenda. And once a day, when you catch yourself gripping, whether a feeling or an outcome, remember the water. Let the grip go for one breath and see whether you sink.',
 'Streaks and ranks are destinations, and they will keep you going for a while. Watts is the frame that makes the four hundredth check-in feel like playing, not like a corridor, which is the only way there is a four hundredth.',
 ARRAY['The backwards law: reaching for security produces insecurity; thrash and you sink, relax and you float.','Life is music, not a journey: the point is the playing, not the final chord.','Acceptance research backs the first idea and intrinsic-motivation research backs the second.'],
 ARRAY['Do one thing today entirely for its own sake, with no result intended and nothing logged.','Next time you catch yourself gripping a feeling or an outcome, let go for one breath and see whether you sink.','Pick one training session this week to treat as the thing itself, not a means to a body.'],
 '[{"author": "Watts, A.", "title": "The Wisdom of Insecurity", "year": 1951}, {"author": "Note", "title": "An original lesson on Watts''s ideas; not affiliated with any estate or publisher"}, {"author": "Hayes, S. et al.", "title": "Experiential avoidance and behavioral disorders: a functional dimensional approach (J Consult Clin Psychol)", "year": 1996}, {"author": "Ryan, R. & Deci, E.", "title": "Self-Determination Theory and the facilitation of intrinsic motivation", "year": 2000}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "What is the backwards law?", "choices": ["Doing things in reverse order", "Reaching for a state signals its absence and keeps it absent; grabbing at security creates insecurity", "Bad things happen to good people"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Thrash and you sink, relax and you float. Acceptance research found the same thing later."}, {"q": "What does Watts mean by life as music rather than a journey?", "choices": ["Nothing matters", "The point is the playing, not arriving at the final chord", "You should listen to more music"], "correct": 1, "explain": "A destination-only life turns every present moment into a corridor. The playing is the point."}]'::jsonb,
 309, 10, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 11 · Recap ───
('wisdom', 'wisdom-practice-stack',
 'The Practice Stack: One Week With All Nine',
 'The recap: nine ideas become one seven-day routine you can run without believing any of it',
 'The course ends as a routine, not a reading list. Morning light, the sixty-second return, one habit cue, a projection check and a Sunday audit: nine teachers folded into a week that takes under twenty minutes a day.',
 'promising', 5,
 ARRAY['One seven-day stack built only from the evidenced practices','Each step tagged with its source lesson so you can go back to the idea when the practice goes stale','A closing honesty note on what this course did not claim'],
 ARRAY['Running all nine at once is the fastest way to run none of them; the stack is a menu with a suggested order, not a contract','The stack is not a treatment plan; if any of it surfaces something heavy, that is the signal to talk to a professional'],
 E'Ten lessons, nine teachers, one rule from Lesson 1: take the practice, test it on a real week, leave the metaphysics on the shelf. Here is the week.\n\n**Every morning, ten minutes**\n- Light outside within an hour of waking, phone in your pocket. (Huberman, Lesson 9)\n- Three physiological sighs before the first hard thing. (Huberman, Lesson 9)\n- One identity sentence, read where you will see it: *I am the kind of person who …* (Clear, Lesson 2)\n\n**Three times a day, sixty seconds**\n- The return: hands from the inside, the furthest sound, one long exhale, *what is here right now?* (Tolle, Lesson 3)\n\n**Whenever it happens**\n- Defensive? Ask: what did I just make part of me? (Tolle, Lesson 4)\n- A hard feeling? Drop the story, find it in the body, *you can be here* for two minutes. (Byrne, Lesson 5)\n- Someone irritating you out of proportion? Write the quality down; ask where it lives in you. (Jung, Lesson 7)\n- Gripping an outcome? Let go for one breath, see whether you sink. (Watts, Lesson 10)\n\n**Before training**\n- Thirty seconds of rehearsal from the inside: the next set, the moment it gets hard, the feeling of it done. (Dispenza, Lesson 8)\n- Cold, if you use it, before the session or on a rest day, never after. (Huberman, Lesson 9)\n\n**Once a day**\n- One thing entirely for its own sake, unlogged. (Watts, Lesson 10)\n\n**Sunday, five minutes**\n- The eight forms, one to five each. Work on the lowest this week. (Sharma, Lesson 6)\n- Never miss twice: look at the week''s misses and make sure none became two. (Clear, Lesson 2)\n\n**How to run it**\nWeek one, run only the morning block and the return. Week two, add the whenever-it-happens tools; they need something to happen. Week three, add the training and Sunday steps. By week four the whole stack takes under twenty minutes of deliberate time, and most of it happens inside things you were already doing.\n\n**What this course did not claim**\nThat thoughts attract outcomes. That meditation cures disease. That the shadow is an entity or that awareness is the substance of the universe. Those claims belong to their authors. What this course claims is smaller and holds up: a habit is designed, attention can step back from a thought, a refused feeling leaks and an allowed one passes, a rehearsed state gets easier, light and breath move physiology, and a life measured on eight scales is richer than a life measured on one. Each of those has evidence next to it in its lesson.\n\n**Where to go next**\nThe Inner Work course holds the deeper versions of rehearsal, self-talk and letting go. The Longevity course holds the decades this stack is for. The Recovery and Nervous System courses hold sleep and NSDR, the two levers this course only pointed at. The Vault is one library; read it as one.',
 'A course is finished when it changes a week, not when the last lesson is marked complete. This stack is the change: nine practices, under twenty minutes, inside the app''s own rhythm of check-ins, sessions and Sundays.',
 ARRAY['Nine teachers become one week: a morning block, a sixty-second return, tools for when things happen, a Sunday audit.','Add the stack in three weeks, not one; the whenever-tools need something to happen to.','This course claimed only what has evidence next to it; the rest was labelled and left on the shelf.'],
 ARRAY['Put the morning block in tomorrow''s calendar: light, three sighs, the identity sentence.','Set a Sunday reminder for the eight-forms audit and the never-miss-twice check.','Mark this lesson complete, then open the Inner Work course; its Lesson 1 is where the next depth is.'],
 '[{"author": "Note", "title": "A synthesis of the ten preceding lessons; each practice is cited in its own lesson"}, {"author": "Clear, J.", "title": "Atomic Habits", "year": 2018}, {"author": "Balban, M.Y. et al.", "title": "Brief structured respiration practices enhance mood and reduce physiological arousal", "year": 2023}, {"author": "Killingsworth, M. & Gilbert, D.", "title": "A wandering mind is an unhappy mind", "year": 2010}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "How should the stack be introduced?", "choices": ["All nine practices from day one", "In three weeks: morning block and the return first, then the whenever-tools, then training and Sunday", "Only on rest days"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Running all nine at once is the fastest way to run none. The whenever-tools also need something to happen to."}, {"q": "Which claim did this course NOT make?", "choices": ["A habit is designed", "Meditation cures disease", "Light and breath move physiology"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The healing claim belongs to its author and was labelled speculative. The course kept only what has evidence next to it."}]'::jsonb,
 310, 11, 'recap')

